Add a "Delete browsing data when you close Aerium" toggle to Settings.

ungoogled-chromium already wires browsing-data-on-exit deletion into
ChromeBrowsingDataLifetimeManager::ClearBrowsingDataForOnExitPolicy() via
extra/ungoogled-chromium/add-flag-to-clear-data-on-exit.patch, but only
behind the #clear-data-on-exit entry in chrome://flags. A flag is not a
feature most people will ever find. This exposes the same shutdown deletion
as an ordinary toggle in Settings > Privacy and security, backed by a pref,
so it survives restarts and shows up in search.

It hangs off the existing code path rather than adding a second one: the new
pref simply widens the condition that already guards the removal.

Two deliberate choices:

  - A new pref instead of writing into kClearBrowsingDataOnExitList. That
    list is owned by the ClearBrowsingDataOnExit enterprise policy, and a
    toggle that rewrote it would look like the browser overriding managed
    configuration. The two sources are ORed at shutdown, so both work.

  - A narrower removal mask than the flag's AllRemoveMask(). That one also
    wipes saved passwords and site settings. A discoverable toggle gets
    switched on by people who want a clean slate each session, and quietly
    destroying their saved passwords and per-site permissions on every quit
    is not what the label promises. The toggle clears history, downloads,
    cookies and site data, cache and form data; the flag keeps its existing,
    more aggressive behaviour untouched.

The pref has to be added to the settingsPrivate allowlist as well, otherwise
the Settings page silently fails to read or write it.

